@flexiarg process-coherence/separate-evidence-history-from-verdict-state
@title Separate Evidence History from Verdict State
@keywords evidence, verdict, provenance, status, append-only, review, overwrite, bitemporal, approval
@audience workflow designers, review-pipeline authors, artifact custodians
@tone corrective
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance slice-1 compression mining (2026-08-12): 7 marks, 4 independent read transcripts, 4 distinct problems (b97A02 b98A03 b00J02 b96J01)

! conclusion:
  Store observations and provenance as append-only evidence, and store current
  approval as a separate, revisable verdict that names the evidence and
  contract version it judged.

  + context:
    Automated checks, human review, migrations, re-freezes, and cross-repo
    merges all update records about the same artifact.  A single status field
    is tempting because every consumer can ask for one latest value.

  + IF:
    Different writers have different authority or semantics: a gate reports an
    observation, a reviewer grants approval, and a migration changes identity
    or contract metadata.

  + HOWEVER:
    Separation alone does not resolve disagreement.  Append-only evidence can
    preserve bad observations forever, while multiple live verdicts can leave
    consumers unsure which one governs.  The design therefore needs explicit
    authority, supersession, and as-of rules; otherwise two tables merely
    replace one ambiguous field.

  + THEN:
    Append each observation with source, time, artifact identity, and contract
    version.  Record verdicts separately with reviewer authority and pointers
    to the evidence judged.  Supersede verdicts explicitly rather than
    overwriting them.  Compute current eligibility from the governing verdict
    and its referenced evidence, never from the last writer to touch a shared
    status cell.

  + BECAUSE:
    Evidence answers what happened; a verdict answers what is currently
    believed or permitted.  Conflating them lets a re-freeze erase a rejection,
    an automated gate overwrite human review, or a merge promote whichever row
    was written last.  Separate histories preserve both auditability and
    revisability.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Inventory status fields written by both automated gates and human reviewers.]
    next[Split observations from verdicts and add explicit evidence and supersession references.]
    next[Test that a bulk metadata refresh cannot change approval eligibility.]
